::ALG2_U01_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 4x = 0. {
~x = -2 and x = 2 #This leaves out x = 0 from the factor x.
=x = -2, 0, 2 #Correct. Factor x(x squared - 4), then x(x - 2)(x + 2) = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 4 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 2, not 4.
~x = -4, 0, 4 #These use the coefficient 4 instead of square roots.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 25x = 0. {
~x = -25, 0, 25 #These values come from the coefficient 25, not from solving x squared - 25 = 0.
=x = -5, 0, 5 #Correct. Factor x(x squared - 25), then x(x - 5)(x + 5) = 0.
~x = -5 and x = 5 #This leaves out x = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 25 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 5.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + 3x<sup>2</sup> = 0. {
~x = -3 only #This leaves out x = 0, which comes from x squared.
=x = 0 and x = -3 #Correct. Factor x squared(x + 3), so x = 0 and x = -3.
~x = 0 and x = 3 #The factor x + 3 gives x = -3.
~x = -1 and x = 3 #These values do not come from the factors.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 9x = 0. {
=x = -3, 0, 3 #Correct. Factor x(x squared - 9), then x(x - 3)(x + 3) = 0.
~x = -3 and x = 3 #This leaves out the solution x = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 9 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 3.
~x = -9, 0, 9 #These values come from 9 instead of its square root.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 8x = 0. {
~x = -2, 0, 2 #This does not solve the quadratic factor x squared plus 2x minus 8.
=x = -4, 0, 2 #Correct. Factor x(x squared + 2x - 8), then x(x + 4)(x - 2) = 0.
~x = -2, 0, 4 #This reverses the signs of the nonzero roots.
~x = -8, 0, 2 #This uses the constant from the quadratic factor incorrectly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x - 6 = 0. {
~x = -1, -2, -3 #These signs do not match the factorization.
=x = 1, 2, 3 #Correct. The polynomial factors as (x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3).
~x = 0, 2, 3 #Zero is not a solution because the constant term is -6.
~x = 1, 3, 6 #These are not all roots of the polynomial.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 5x - 6 = 0, given that x = -1 is a solution. {
~x = -1 only #A cubic equation can have more than one solution.
=x = -3, -1, 2 #Correct. Dividing by x + 1 gives x squared + x - 6, which factors as (x + 3)(x - 2).
~x = -2, 1, 3 #These are the opposite signs of the roots.
~x = -3, 1, 2 #This includes 1 instead of -1.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - 4x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 = 0, given that x = 2 is a solution. {
~x = 2 only #The known solution is only the starting point.
=x = -1, 2, 3 #Correct. Dividing by x - 2 gives x squared - 2x - 3, so the remaining roots are -1 and 3.
~x = -3, 1, 2 #These do not match the quadratic factor after division.
~x = -2, 1, 3 #This set does not include the known solution x = 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> - 16 = 0 over the complex number system. {
~x = -2 and x = 2 #These are only the real solutions.
=x = -2, 2, -2i, 2i #Correct. Factor into (x - 2)(x + 2)(x squared + 4).
~x = -4, 4, -4i, 4i #These use 16 instead of the square root relationship.
~x = -2i and x = 2i #These are only the complex non-real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> - 81 = 0 over the complex number system. {
~x = -9 and x = 9 #These are not the real roots of x to the fourth equals 81.
=x = -3, 3, -3i, 3i #Correct. Factor as (x squared - 9)(x squared + 9), then solve each factor.
~x = -9, 9, -9i, 9i #These values come from 81 instead of the square root structure.
~x = -3i and x = 3i #These are only the complex non-real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> + x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 4 = 0. {
~x = -1, -2, 2 #The quadratic factor gives complex roots, not real roots -2 and 2.
=x = -1, -2i, 2i #Correct. Group to get (x + 1)(x squared + 4) = 0.
~x = 1, -2i, 2i #The linear factor gives x = -1.
~x = -4 and x = 4 #These values do not solve the equation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>3</sup> - x<sup>2</sup> + 9x - 9 = 0. {
~x = 1, -3, 3 #The quadratic factor is x squared plus 9, which gives complex roots.
=x = 1, -3i, 3i #Correct. Group to get (x - 1)(x squared + 9) = 0.
~x = -1, -3i, 3i #The linear factor x - 1 gives x = 1.
~x = 9 and x = -9 #These values do not come from the factors.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution has multiplicity 2 in (x + 4)(x - 1)<sup>2</sup> = 0? {
~x = -4 #The factor x + 4 appears once.
=x = 1 #Correct. The factor x - 1 appears twice, so x = 1 has multiplicity 2.
~x = -1 #The factor x - 1 gives x = 1, not -1.
~x = 4 #The factor x + 4 gives x = -4, not 4.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution has multiplicity 3 in (x + 2)<sup>3</sup>(x - 5) = 0? {
=x = -2 #Correct. The factor x + 2 appears three times, so x = -2 has multiplicity 3.
~x = 2 #The factor x + 2 gives x = -2.
~x = 5 #The factor x - 5 appears once.
~x = -5 #The factor x - 5 gives x = 5.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step that best preserves all solutions when solving x<sup>3</sup> - 16x = 0? {
~Divide both sides by x #This can lose the solution x = 0.
=Factor out x #Correct. Factoring gives x(x squared - 16) = 0 and keeps x = 0 visible.
~Take the cube root of 16 #The equation has two terms with x and should be factored.
~Add 16 to both sides #This does not isolate or factor the polynomial correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>If x = 4 is a zero of a polynomial, which linear factor is associated with that zero? {
~x + 4 #This factor gives the zero x = -4.
=x - 4 #Correct. The zero x = 4 corresponds to the factor x - 4.
~4x - 1 #This factor gives x = 1/4.
~x/4 #This factor gives x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>If x = -6 is a zero of a polynomial, which linear factor is associated with that zero? {
=x + 6 #Correct. The zero x = -6 corresponds to the factor x + 6.
~x - 6 #This factor gives x = 6.
~6x - 1 #This factor gives x = 1/6.
~x/6 #This factor gives x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>A graph of a polynomial shows x-intercepts at -2, 1, and 4. Which list gives the real solutions? {
~x = 2, -1, -4 #These are the opposite signs of the x-intercepts.
=x = -2, 1, 4 #Correct. Real zeros appear as x-intercepts.
~x = -2i, i, 4i #Imaginary values do not appear as x-intercepts.
~x = -4, -1, 2 #These do not match the intercepts.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Why might a graph not show every solution of a polynomial equation? {
~Because real solutions never appear on graphs #Real solutions do appear as x-intercepts.
=Because complex non-real solutions are not x-intercepts #Correct. A real-coordinate graph shows real zeros, not complex non-real zeros.
~Because polynomial equations have no roots #Polynomial equations can have roots.
~Because x-intercepts are always imaginary #X-intercepts are real coordinate points.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve (x - 2)(x + 7)(x<sup>2</sup> + 1) = 0. {
~x = 2 and x = -7 #This leaves out the complex solutions from x squared plus 1.
=x = 2, -7, i, -i #Correct. The linear factors give 2 and -7, and x squared plus 1 gives plus or minus i.
~x = -2, 7, i, -i #The real roots have the wrong signs.
~x = 1 and x = -1 #These do not solve x squared plus 1 = 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve (x + 3)(x<sup>2</sup> - 4) = 0. {
~x = 3, -2, 2 #The factor x + 3 gives x = -3.
=x = -3, -2, 2 #Correct. Solve x + 3 = 0 and x squared - 4 = 0.
~x = -3, -4, 4 #The quadratic factor gives plus or minus 2, not 4.
~x = -2 and x = 2 #This leaves out the solution from x + 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve (x - 1)(x<sup>2</sup> + 9) = 0. {
~x = 1, -3, 3 #The quadratic factor x squared plus 9 gives complex roots.
=x = 1, -3i, 3i #Correct. x - 1 gives 1, and x squared plus 9 gives plus or minus 3i.
~x = -1, -3i, 3i #The linear factor gives x = 1, not -1.
~x = 9 and x = -9 #These do not solve either factor.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> - 5x<sup>2</sup> + 4 = 0. {
~x = -4, -1, 1, 4 #These values come from factors of 4, not the quadratic in x squared.
=x = -2, -1, 1, 2 #Correct. Let u = x squared, factor u squared - 5u + 4 as (u - 1)(u - 4), then solve.
~x = -2 and x = 2 #This leaves out the roots from x squared = 1.
~x = -1 and x = 1 #This leaves out the roots from x squared = 4.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>4</sup> + 5x<sup>2</sup> + 4 = 0 over the complex number system. {
~x = -2, -1, 1, 2 #These would solve x to the fourth minus 5x squared plus 4.
=x = i, -i, 2i, -2i #Correct. Let u = x squared, factor u squared + 5u + 4, then x squared = -1 or -4.
~x = -4, -1, 1, 4 #These are real values and do not solve the equation.
~x = i and x = -i #This leaves out the solutions from x squared = -4.
}

::ALG2_U01_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.7<br><br>Question:<br>What is the best reason to use polynomial division when solving a cubic equation with one known solution? {
~It changes every complex solution into a real solution. #Polynomial division does not change the solution type.
=It reduces the cubic to a quadratic that can be solved. #Correct. Dividing by the known linear factor reduces the degree.
~It removes the need to check any solutions. #Solutions should still be checked or connected to factors.
~It makes the remainder the only solution. #The remainder is not the solution set.
}
